\h Romaios
\toc1 Romaios
\toc2 Romaios
\toc3 Rom.
\mt2 Paulos’s letter to the believers in
\mt1 Romai
\is1 Introduction
\ip \bk Paulos’s letter to the believers in Romai\bk* was sent to both Yehudim and non-Jews who believe in, and trust, Yeshua (Jesus) the messiah. He wrote to them so that they could be prepared for when he could visit them there. Paulos planned to work there with other Christians for a while, then with their help, he would travel to Spania. He wrote in order to explain his understanding of Christian faith and of day-to-day living as Christians.
\ip Paulos greets the believers there in Romai and he tells them about his prayers for them. Then he tells them the theme of his letter, “how God makes us guiltless—by faith.” (1:17a). All people, Jewish or not, need to become guiltless in God’s sight by means of faith in Yeshua Messiah.
\ip Next Paulos tells them about new life by means of unity with the messiah thereby forming a new relationship with God. Believers will have peace from God and the power of Spirit of God will save them from the power of sin and of death. In chapters 5-8, Paulos explains about the purpose of God’s law and about the power of God’s spirit working through believers.
\ip Paulos answers the question about how Yehudim and non-Jews fit into God’s plan for of all people. Paulos explains that the unbelief of Yehudim is part of God’s plan for a time so that the good message can be proclaimed to non-Jews. But concerning God’s choosing of Yehudim, he loved them very much due to his promise to their ancestors, and it’s as a result of them that all people can receive the love and mercy of God through the messiah Yeshua.
\ip In the ending, Paulos explains how to live as a Christian, with the main need being to accept and love each other. He also includes themes like serving God, the responsibility of Christians the government, and for each individual, questions concerning the conscience. Paulos ends his letter and greeting others, and by his praising God.
\iot Main components of Paulos’s letter
\io1 Introduction and theme \ior 1:1-17\ior*
\io1 All people need salvation \ior 1:18–3:20\ior*
\io1 God’s way of saving people \ior 3:21–4:25\ior*
\io1 The new life in the messiah \ior 5:1–8:39\ior*
\io1 God’s plan for Yisrael \ior 9:1–11:36\ior*
\io1 Christian living \ior 12:1–15:13\ior*
\io1 Concluding remarks and greetings \ior 15:14–16:27\ior*
\rem This is still an early look into the unpolished text of the Open English Translation of the Bible. Please double-check the text in advance before using in public.
\rem NOTE: This USFM file was exported from the ESFM source file with some loss of features, so don’t edit this derived file.
\c 1
\p
\v 1 \add This letter is from\add* Paulos, a slave \nd of Messiah Yeshua\nd* \add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\add* and a \nd chosen\nd* ambassador who’s been set apart for \nd God’s\nd* good message,
\v 2 which \add God\add* had previously promised through \add the messages\add* of his prophets in the holy scriptures.
\p
\v 3 \add This good message\add* is about his son, who was conceived as a descendant of David,
\v 4 and became known as \nd God’s\nd* son according to the \nd spirit\nd* of holiness—demonstrating power by \add coming back to life\add* from the dead. Yes, he’s our \nd master\nd*, \nd Yeshua Messiah\nd*,
\v 5 and it’s through him that we received generous gifts, and \add also\add* the assignment to faithfully, obediently \add take\add* his name to all the non-Jews,
\v 6 which also includes you non-Jews there who \nd Yeshua Messiah\nd* invited.
\p
\v 7 \add This letter is written\add* to all those in Romai that \nd God\nd* loves and considers as holy. \add May\add* you all experience generosity and peace from \nd God\nd* our father and \add from\add* \nd the master Yeshua Messiah\nd*.
\s1 Paulos’s wish to visit Romai
\p
\v 8 I’ll start by thanking my \nd God\nd* through \nd Yeshua \+add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\+add* Messiah\nd* for all of you, because the \add story about\add* your faith has been told in many countries.
\v 9 Yes, the \nd God\nd* that I serve in my spirit, in the good message about his son, is my witness about how I never stop mentioning you \add Romaios\add*
\v 10 in my prayers—asking that somehow \nd God\nd* will favour me by allowing me to come and visit you,
\v 11 because I’m longing to see you so that I can share a particular spiritual gift with you in order to strengthen you.
\v 12 I want \add all of\add* us to be encouraged together by each other’s faith—both me and you \add Romaios\add*.
\p
\v 13 \add Brothers and sisters\add*, I don’t want you to be \add unaware\add* that I’d planned several times to come to you,\x + \xo 1:13: \xt Acts 19:21.\x* but I was vetoed until recently so that I’d also have a harvest among you similar to those among the other non-Jews.
\v 14 Whether it’s Hellains \add (Greeks)\add* or other foreigners, wise \add people\add* or foolish, I have an obligation,
\v 15 and so I’m also eager to talk about the good message to all of you there in Romai.
\p
\v 16 I’m not ashamed to talk about the good message,\x + \xo 1:16: \xt Mrk 8:38.\x* because it \add conveys\add* \nd God’s\nd* power to save everyone who believes it—first Yehudim \add (Jews)\add* and then non-Jews.
\v 17 It explains how \nd God\nd* makes us guiltless—by faith \add leading\add* to \add more\add* faith—as it’s been written: Godly \add people\add* will live by faith.\x + \xo 1:17: \xt Hab 2:4.\x*
\s1 God’s anger towards those who deny his existence
\p
\v 18 \nd God’s\nd* severe anger is being revealed from heaven on the ungodliness and disobedience of people who limit the truth by \add their\add* disobedience,
\v 19 because the fact that there is a \nd god\nd* has been shown to them, because \nd God\nd* \add himself\add* has revealed it.
\v 20 Ever since the creation of the world, \add God’s\add* invisible attributes have been clearly seen through his workmanship—both his eternal power and his divinity—so \add people\add* have no excuse.
\v 21 Even though they know \add about\add* \nd God\nd*, they didn’t honour or thank \add him\add*, but their theories made them incompetent and their minds turned from perception towards darkness.\x + \xo 1:21: \xt Eph 4:17-18.\x*
\v 22 They claimed to be scientific, but instead they believed \add foolishness\add*,
\v 23 and \add so\add* they changed the greatness of the eternal \nd God\nd* into the likeness of an image of a mortal human, or birds or animals or reptiles.\x + \xo 1:23: \xt Deu 4:16-18.\x*
\p
\v 24 Therefore \nd God\nd* reluctantly let their lusts lead to impurity and the dishonouring of their bodies among themselves,
\v 25 as they exchanged \nd God’s\nd* truth for a lie, and worshipped and served the creation, instead of the creator, who \add should be\add* praised throughout all time. \add May it be so\add*.
\p
\v 26 Because of that, \nd God\nd* reluctantly let them exercise their dishonourable passions, and their women exchanged natural behaviours for unnatural ones,
\v 27 and similarly their men deserted the natural behaviours towards women, and stoked their lusts towards each other—man with man, and the shame that it produces, and then received back in themselves the payment which is appropriate for such deception.
\p
\v 28 They didn’t want to know about \nd God\nd*, and so \add he\add* reluctantly let \add their\add* depraved \add minds\add* be filled with unhelpful thoughts,
\v 29 and so they were filled with disobedience, wickedness, greed, and malice, and full of envy, murder, strife, and deceit, \add becoming\add* malicious people, gossips,
\v 30 slanderers, god-haters, abusers, proud \add people\add*, braggers, and inventors of evil, unable to be persuaded by \add their\add* parents,
\v 31 unintelligent, untrustworthy, unloving, \add and\add* unmerciful.
\v 32 They know that \nd God\nd* must exercise justice towards people doing such things, yet they not only practice those things which will lead to a death sentence, but they also approve when others do them.
\c 2
\s1 Judgement even for those without the written law
\p
\v 1 So there’s no excuse for anyone who judges others,\x + \xo 2:1: \xt Mat 7:1; Luk 6:37.\x* because you’re condemning yourselves if you’re doing those same \add things\add* that you’re judging others for,
\v 2 because we know that \nd God’s\nd* judgement is based on the factual accounts \nd of\nd* what \add each person\add* is doing.
\v 3 But maybe you’re thinking that you can judge others doing these things and yet \add somehow\add* escape \nd God’s\nd* judgement when you do them yourself?
\v 4 Or maybe you’re \add taking advantage\add* \nd of God’s\nd* kindness and patience, without realising that his kindness was \add meant\add* to lead you to \add turn away from your wrongdoing\add*?
\v 5 But it’s your hardness and failure to \add turn away from wrongdoing\add* that’s \add causing\add* severe anger to be stored up for \nd God’s\nd* judgement day, when \add all\add* \add will be revealed\add*, \add including\add* his severe anger,
\v 6 as each person will be \add judged\add* by their actions.\x + \xo 2:6: \xt Psa 62:12; Prv 24:12.\x*
\v 7 Those who’ve \add persistently\add* done good, expecting praise and honour, and to escape destruction, \add will be given\add* eternal life,
\v 8 but those who’ve \add lived out\add* \add their\add* selfish ambition and refused to believe the truth, but \add let themselves get\add* persuaded by disobedience, \add will meet with\add* severe anger and rage.
\v 9 For every person doing evil, there’ll be trouble and distress—to Yehudim \add (Jews)\add* first and then to non-Jews,
\v 10 but for those who do good, there’ll be praise and honour and peace—to Yehudim first and then to non-Jews,
\v 11 because \nd God\nd* doesn’t \add indulge in\add* favouritism.\x + \xo 2:11: \xt Deu 10:17.\x*
\p
\v 12 Those who weren’t under the \add Jewish\add* laws and yet sinned, they’ll be judged without the law, and those who knew the laws and sinned will be judged by the law,
\v 13 because listening to the law doesn’t make \add people\add* right with \nd God\nd*, but rather it’s those who obey the law who’ll be declared guiltless.
\v 14 Whenever other nations don’t have \add the Jewish\add* laws, but naturally obey what’s in the laws, \add they show that\add* even without \add our\add* laws, they have their own law.
\v 15 They demonstrate that they effectively have \add God’s\add* law written in their \add minds\add* and agreeing with their \add consciences\add*, and between the two they’ll be either accused or defended
\v 16 on the day when \nd God\nd*, through \nd Messiah Yeshua\nd* \add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\add*, judges people’s private \add lives\add*, as I’ve shared \add as part of\add* the good message.
\s1 Yehudim who disobey their laws
\p
\v 17 But if you’re a Yehudi \add (Jew)\add*, and trusting in the law, and boasting about \add being chosen by\add* \nd God\nd*
\v 18 and knowing his will and knowing what’s important because of familiarity with the law,
\v 19 and if you’ve persuaded yourself that you’re a guide for blind \add people\add* and a light to people who are in darkness,
\v 20 and that you’re an instructor to foolish \add people\add* and a teacher of children, and you \add appear\add* to be knowledgeable about the truth of the law,
\v 21 then why do you teach others when you haven’t \add even\add* taught yourself? You announce that stealing is wrong, but you’re a thief.
\v 22 You teach about not messing with other people’s spouses, yet you do it yourself. You detest idols, but you sneak money out of the temple.
\v 23 You boast about \add being a student\add* of the law, but you dishonour \nd God\nd* by breaking the laws.
\v 24 \add Yes,\add* it’s been written: ‘\nd God’s\nd* name is slandered among the nations because of you \add people\add*.’\x + \xo 2:24: \xt Isa 52:5 (LXX).\x*
\p
\v 25 Circumcision is of value if you’re keeping the law, but if you’re breaking the laws, then your circumcision means just the same as if you’re \add uncircumcised\add*,
\v 26 so if \add uncircumcised\add* \add people groups\add* behave justly according to the law, even though they’re not physically circumcised, \add God\add* will consider them as his people.
\v 27 \add Yes, those people groups\add* who aren’t circumcised, but keep the law, will be judging you who were given the law in writing and \add are circumcised\add*, yet break those laws.
\v 28 A \add true\add* Yehudi isn’t just a Yehudi on the outside, and \add their\add* circumcision isn’t just on the outside of \add their\add* body,
\v 29 but they’re a Yehudi even in their private life and through circumcision of \add their\add* heart\x + \xo 2:29: \xt Deu 30:6.\x*—by \nd the spirit\nd* and not by the letter of the law—earning praise not from people, but from \nd God\nd*.
\c 3
\p
\v 1 So what advantage is there in being a Yehudi \add (Jew)\add*, and what’s the benefit of circumcision?
\v 2 A lot, in every way. Firstly, \add Yehudim\add* \add (Jews)\add* were entrusted with \add the writing down\add* \nd of God’s\nd* messages.
\v 3 But what if some didn’t believe? Wouldn’t the unbelief of \add Yehudim\add* make faith \add that comes from\add* \nd God\nd* worthless?
\v 4 Absolutely not. Even if every person was a liar, \nd God\nd* is truthful, just like it’s been written \add about him\add*:
\q1 ‘You will be proved right about what you say,
\q2 and you’ll prevail when you’re judged \add in court\add*.’\x + \xo 3:4: \xt Psa 51:4 (LXX).\x*
\p
\v 5 But if our sinfulness highlights \nd God’s\nd* sinlessness, what can we say about that? Isn’t \nd God\nd*, the one inflicting the severe anger, wrong \add to punish us\add*? (Of course, this is reasoning from a human perspective.)
\v 6 Of course not, otherwise how could \nd God\nd* be able to judge the world?
\p
\v 7 But if my lie \add exposes\add* \nd God’s\nd* truth and so brings him honour, why would he still judge me as a sinner?
\v 8 Some people falsely accuse us of saying, ‘Let’s do evil things so that good might follow.’ Those who say that deserve their punishment.
\s1 No person is guiltless
\p
\v 9 What then? Do \add Yehudim\add* \add (Jews)\add* have any advantage? Definitely not, because we showed earlier that both Yehudim and other nationalities are all under sin’s \add shadow\add*,
\v 10 just like it was written long ago:
\q1 ‘No one is guiltless,
\q2 not even one person.
\q1
\v 11 No one understands,
\q2 there’s not even one person trying to find \nd God\nd*.
\q1
\v 12 Everyone has turned away,
\q2 it was a waste of time.
\q1 No one is being kind,
\q2 Not even one person.’\x + \xo 3:10-12: \xt Psa 14:1-3 (LXX); Psa 53:1-3 (LXX).\x*
\q1
\v 13 ‘Their words lead to death,
\q2 their tongues are deceptive.’\x + \xo 3:13: \xt Psa 5:9 (LXX).\x*
\q1 ‘Their lips spray out poison.’\x + \xo 3:13: \xt Psa 140:3.\x*
\q1
\v 14 ‘Their \add mouths\add* are full of cursing and bitterness.’\x + \xo 3:14: \xt Psa 10:7 (LXX).\x*
\q1
\v 15 ‘They rush off to go and shed blood.
\q2
\v 16 They leave trails of destruction and misery—
\q2
\v 17 they don’t know the path \add that leads to\add* peace.’\x + \xo 3:15-17: \xt Isa 59:7-8.\x*
\q2
\v 18 Revering \nd God\nd* isn’t at the front of their \add minds\add*.\x + \xo 3:18: \xt Psa 36:1.\x*
\p
\v 19 We know that whatever the law says, it’s for \add everyone\add* that the law applies to, so that no one can speak against it, and the whole world will be bound by \nd God’s\nd* judgement.
\v 20 No human will be declared guiltless \add in front of\add* \add God\add* by their keeping the law,\x + \xo 3:20: \xt Psa 143:2; Gal 2:16.\x* because it’s the law that teaches what sin is.
\s1 Removing the guilt from sin
\p
\v 21 But now \nd God\nd* has revealed a way to remove guilt that’s not by rules—a way that Mosheh \add (Moses)\add* and the prophets wrote \nd about\nd*.
\v 22 We can be made \add right\add* with \nd God\nd* through faith in \nd Yeshua \+add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\+add* Messiah\nd*.\x + \xo 3:22: \xt Gal 2:16.\x* This is for \em everyone\em* who believes, because there’s no distinction \add between Yehudim\add* \add (Jews)\add* \add and others\add*,
\v 23 because everyone has sinned and is unable to be honoured by \nd God\nd*.
\v 24 \add But\add* even without deserving it, we can be declared guiltless by \add God’s\add* generosity, by \add being bought out of slavery\add* \add to sin\add* by \nd Messiah Yeshua\nd*,
\v 25 who \nd God\nd* placed \add on the altar\add* as a sacrifice for \add sin\add*. \add This happens\add* through faith in his blood, which was a sign of his sinlessness.
\p (God hasn’t yet judged those who sinned in the past,
\v 26 because \nd of\nd* \add his\add* patience, waiting for the present time to show his righteousness and to be fair, and to remove the guilt of \add those\add* with faith \nd in Yeshua\nd*.)
\p
\v 27 So where does that leave boasting? It’s not in the picture. How come? Through obeying \add Mosheh’s\add* laws? No, through a law about faith.
\v 28 So now we should expect that a person will be made guiltless by faith—not by obeying \add Mosheh’s\add* \add laws\add*.
\v 29 Or is \nd God\nd* only god of the Yehudim? Isn’t he also the \nd god\nd* of others? Yes, he is the god of others.
\v 30 Indeed, there’s only one \nd God\nd*,\x + \xo 3:30: \xt Deu 6:4; Gal 3:20.\x* and he declares people guiltless by faith whether they’re \add circumcised\add* Yehudim, or non-Jews.
\v 31 So does this ‘faith’ cancel the law? Not on your life. In fact, it upholds the law.
\c 4
\s1 Avraham’s faith
\p
\v 1 So what did our physical ancestor, Avraham, discover about all this?
\v 2 Because if Avraham was declared guiltless because of \add his\add* actions, maybe he could boast? But not towards \nd God\nd*,
\v 3 because the \add scriptures\add* say: ‘Avraham believed \nd in God\nd*, so he was considered guiltless.’\x + \xo 4:3: \xt Gen 15:6; Gal 3:6.\x*
\v 4 Now for someone who’s working, their wages are considered ‘owed’, not a gift.
\v 5 However, someone who’s not working, but \add having faith\add* that \add God\add* will declare disobedient people as guiltless, that person’s faith is considered to make them guiltless.
\v 6 It’s just like what David said about blessing a person who \nd God\nd* declares as guiltless despite \add their\add* actions:
\q1
\v 7 ‘Those who’ve been forgiven for their lawlessness will be happy,
\q2 whose sins have been covered over.
\q1
\v 8 Anyone who \nd Yahweh\nd* declares to have no sin will be happy.’\x + \xo 4:7-8: \xt Psa 32:1-2.\x*
\p
\v 9 Is this favour shown \add only\add* to \add circumcised\add* Yehudim \add (Jews)\add*, or is it also for non-Jews? After all, we were saying that Avraham was considered guiltless because of \add his\add* faith.
\v 10 So how did that happen? Because he \add was circumcised\add*, or when he was \add still\add* \add uncircumcised\add*? No, it was before he \add was circumcised\add*.
\v 11 He received circumcision as a sign\x + \xo 4:11: \xt Gen 17:10.\x*—proof of the righteousness that he gained from \add his\add* faith while he was still \add uncircumcised\add*—becoming the father of everyone who’s \add uncircumcised\add* but believes so that they’ll be declared guiltless.
\v 12 \add So\add* our ancestor Avraham is the ancestor of us as a \add circumcised\add* people group, not just because we’re \add circumcised\add*, but also as we follow the steps of faith he took while \add uncircumcised\add*.
\s1 God’s promise is attained through faith
\p
\v 13 It wasn’t because of \add his obeying\add* the law that it was promised that Avraham or his descendants would inherit the world, but because of \add his\add* righteousness \add that came from\add* \add his\add* faith,\x + \xo 4:13: \xt Gen 17:4-6; 22:17-18; Gal 3:29.\x*
\v 14 because if the ones \add obeying\add* the law \add can become\add* heirs, then faith would be declared as useless, and the promise would be invalidated,\x + \xo 4:14: \xt Gal 3:18.\x*
\v 15 because the law produces severe anger, and where there’s no law, there’s nothing to break.
\p
\v 16 Because of this, the promise to the \add descendants\add* is by faith—\add given\add* not only to the ‘\add descendants\add* of the law’, but also to the ‘\add descendants\add* of Avraham’s faith’.\x + \xo 4:16: \xt Gal 3:7.\x* This promise \add is given as a gift\add*, and has been confirmed,
\v 17 as it’s been written, ‘I’ve appointed you as a father of many nations.’\x + \xo 4:17: \xt Gen 17:5.\x* He was appointed in front of the god he believed in—the god who brings the dead \add back\add* to life, and who \add creates by\add* speaking things into being that didn’t previously exist.
\v 18 \add Without any other evidence\add*, he believed in hope itself, and thus he became the father of many nations, just like he’d been told: ‘Your descendants will be like \add the sand and the stars\add*.’\x + \xo 4:18: \xt Gen 15:5.\x*
\v 19 He didn’t falter in his faith, seeing that his body was already infertile, being about a hundred years old, and with Sarah being past menopause.\x + \xo 4:19: \xt Gen 17:17.\x*
\v 20 He didn’t show unbelief and doubt \nd God’s\nd* promise when it was given, but rather he was strengthened in \add his\add* faith and praised \nd God\nd*,
\v 21 being quite certain that what \add God\add* promises, he’s also able to do.
\v 22 Therefore \add his faith\add* was credited to him and he was declared guiltless.
\v 23 However, that statement wasn’t written about \add Avraham\add* only,
\v 24 but about us, too, who are also going to receive the same credit, because of our faith in the \add God\add* who brought our \nd master Yeshua\nd* \add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\add* back to life from the dead
\v 25 after he was handed over \add to the executioners\add* because of our sins, and he was \add brought back to life\add* to make us guiltless.\x + \xo 4:25: \xt Isa 53:4-5.\x*
\c 5
\s1 God showed his love to sinners
\p
\v 1 So now because we’ve been declared guiltless by \add our\add* faith, we can have peace with \nd God\nd* through our \nd master Yeshua \+add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\+add* Messiah\nd*.
\v 2 Through him we also have access by faith into this generosity that we \add depend on\add*, and we’re boasting in hope of \nd God’s\nd* glory. WHAT DOES THIS MEAN?
\v 3 Not only that, but we’re also boasting even though we’re \add still\add* being persecuted, because we know that difficulties produce endurance,
\v 4 and endurance shows who we really are, and knowing that produces hope,
\v 5 and we’re not disgraced by that hope, because \nd God’s\nd* love has been poured out into our hearts through \nd the\nd* holy \nd spirit\nd* who’s been given to us.
\p
\v 6 While we were still weak, and at the \add right\add* time for ungodly \add people\add*, \nd the messiah\nd* died for us.
\v 7 Now it’s rare for someone to \add offer to\add* die to save a guiltless person, although perhaps someone might offer for the \add greater\add* good,
\v 8 but \nd God\nd* demonstrated his love towards us in that \nd the messiah\nd* died for us \em while we were still sinners\em*!
\p
\v 9 So now that we’ve been declared guiltless because of his blood, we’ll certainly be spared from \add his\add* severe anger,
\v 10 because if we were reconciled \nd to God\nd* through the \em death\em* of his son \em while we were still enemies\em*, now that we’ve been reconciled, how much more certain that we’ll be saved by \add Yeshua’s\add* \add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\add* \em life\em*!
\v 11 And not only that, because of what our \nd master Yeshua Messiah\nd* \add has done\add* so that we might receive that reconciliation, we can now boast in \nd God\nd* about it.
\s1 Death through Adam but life through Yeshua
\p
\v 12 Sin came into the world through one man,\x + \xo 5:12: \xt Gen 3:6.\x* and death came as a result of sin, so death spread to everyone because everyone has disobeyed God.
\v 13 Sin was in the world before \add Mosheh’s\add* \add (Moses)\add* law, but it wasn’t being charged against them, because the law had not yet \add been given\add*.
\v 14 But death reigned from \add Adam’s time\add* until \add Mosheh’s time\add*, even over those who didn’t sin in the way that Adam did—Adam\f + \fr 5:14 \ft Adam is called ‘the son of God’ in Luke 3:38.\f* who was a pattern of the \add messiah\add* who would \add eventually\add* come.
\p
\v 15 But the gift is not like the disobedience, because if the disobedience of one man introduced death for many, \nd God’s\nd* generosity and the gift that comes from that generosity is much more. That gift is the one man \nd Yeshua \+add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\+add* Messiah\nd*, who’s been given to very many \add people\add*.
\v 16 It only took one man who disobeyed \add to lead to\add* condemnation, and one act of disobedience \add to lead to\add* judgement, but the gift is to bring guiltlessness despite \em many\em* acts of disobedience.
\v 17 Because if the disobedience of one man led to death reigning \add over us\add* through that one man, how much more will the ones receiving an abundance of generosity and the gift of righteousness be reigning in life through the one man \nd Yeshua Messiah\nd*.
\p
\v 18 So just as one act of disobedience \add led\add* all people to condemnation, so also one action \add brought\add* all people to guiltlessness \add and hence to\add* life.
\v 19 The disobedience of one man caused many people to be made sinners, so also the obedience of one man caused many people to be made guiltless.
\p
\v 20 The law was introduced so that acts of disobedience would be plentiful, but where sin has been plentiful, generosity has overflowed,
\v 21 so that just as sin reigned in death, so also generosity \add now\add* reigns through righteousness, \add leading to\add* eternal life through our \nd master Yeshua Messiah\nd*.
\c 6
\s1 Dying and coming back to life with Yeshua
\p
\v 1 So should we continue disobeying so that more of \add God’s\add* generosity \add will be displayed\add*?
\v 2 Not on your life! We who died to sin, how could we \add go back\add* to living in it?
\v 3 Or didn’t you realise that when we were immersed into \nd Messiah Yeshua\nd* \add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\add*, we were immersed into his death?
\v 4 Therefore we were buried with him through the immersion into death, so that just as \nd Messiah\nd* was \add brought back to life\add* by the father’s power, so too we can \add live a new\add* life.\x + \xo 6:4: \xt Col 2:12.\x*
\v 5 If we’ve \add taken part\add* in the likeness of his death, then we’ll also \add take part\add* in the resurrection,
\v 6 knowing that the old personality was executed with him so that sin would have no \add more\add* power over our \add bodies\add*, and hence we no longer need to serve it,
\v 7 because the personality that’s died has been declared free from sin.
\v 8 \add So\add* if we died with \nd Messiah\nd*, we also believe that we’ll live with him,
\v 9 knowing that now that \nd Messiah\nd* has \add come back to life\add* from the dead, he won’t die again—death \add has no more power over\add* him.
\v 10 When he died, he died to sin once and for all, but now that he lives, he lives \nd to\nd* \add please\add* \nd God\nd*.
\v 11 So too, you should consider yourselves dead to sin, and at the same time, alive \nd to God\nd* in \nd Messiah Yeshua\nd*.
\p
\v 12 So therefore don’t let sin rule over your mortal \add bodies\add* in such a way that you’re submitting to its lusts.
\v 13 Don’t use any parts of your bodies to sin, as if they’re weapons of disobedience, but instead present yourselves \nd to God\nd* as if you \add came back to life\add* from the dead, and use all your \add body parts\add* for God, as if they’re weapons of righteousness,
\v 14 so that sin won’t \add take control\add* of you—because you’re not under law, but under generosity.
\s1 Now we are slaves to righteousness
\p
\v 15 What could that mean? That we can sin \add freely\add* because we’re not under law but under generosity? Not on your life!
\v 16 Don’t you know who you’re presenting yourselves to as \add obedient\add* slaves? \add Yes,\add* whoever you submit to, you’re their slaves—either \add slaves\add* of sin that leads to death, or \add slaves\add* of obedience that leads to righteousness.
\v 17 But \add be thankful\add* \nd to God\nd* that \add although\add* you were slaves to sin, \add your\add* \add hearts\add* submitted to what was taught,
\v 18 and \add so\add*, having been freed from sin, you became slaves of righteousness.
\v 19 I’m speaking from a human perspective because of your \add human\add* weakness. Just as you previously used parts of your bodies as slaves to impurity and lawlessness, leading \add down to further\add* lawlessness, so now present your \add body parts\add* as slaves to righteousness, leading \add up\add* to holiness.
\p
\v 20 When you were slaves of sin, you were immune to righteousness,
\v 21 and so what was the fruit you had back then which you’re now ashamed of? The end \add result\add* of that \add lifestyle\add* is death.
\v 22 But now that you’re free from \add slavery to\add* sin, and are \nd God’s\nd* slaves \add instead\add*, you bear the fruit of holiness, and the end \add result\add* of that \add is living\add* forever,
\v 23 because the wages of sin are death, but \nd God\nd* gives eternal life as a gift through our \nd master Yeshua \+add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\+add* Messiah\nd*.
\c 7
\s1 Becoming dead to the Law
\p
\v 1 \add Brothers and sisters\add*, you know the Law \add well\add* that I’m talking about, so surely you realise that the law acts as a person’s master for as long as they live?
\v 2 For \add example\add*, a woman who’s married to her husband is bound by the law, but if her husband dies then she’s released from the law concerning her husband.
\v 3 So while her husband was still living, he’d call her an adulteress if she \add slept\add* with another man, but after her husband is deceased, she’s free from the law and doesn’t become an adulteress if she \add marries\add* another man.
\v 4 So, my \add brothers and sisters\add*, you, too, were made dead to the law through the body of the \nd messiah\nd* so that you could \add join\add* to another—to the \nd one\nd* who \add came back to life\add* from the dead—so that we can bear fruit for \nd God\nd*.
\v 5 When we were \add still\add* focused on the physical, the passions \add aroused\add* by sinning against the law were working in our \add body parts\add* so that the fruit would be death,
\v 6 but now that we’re released from the law which held us—released by dying to it—we can serve in \add new\add* ways \add guided by the\add* \nd spirit\nd* instead of by the letter \add of the law\add*.
\s1 God’s Law reveals our sin
\p
\v 7 So what will we say then? That the law is sin? Not on your life! I wouldn’t have known what sin was if it wasn’t for the Law. \add For example,\add* I wouldn’t have known what coveting was if the Law hadn’t told me not to covet.\x + \xo 7:7: \xt Exo 20:17; Deu 5:21.\x*
\v 8 But sin took the opportunity from \add that\add* command to produce covetousness in me, because sin would be dead without the Law.
\v 9 For a time I was living without the Law, but once \add God’s\add* command came, sin revived
\v 10 and I died, and I discovered that the command, which \add should lead to\add* life, \add led\add* to death.
\v 11 Sin deceived me\x + \xo 7:11: \xt Gen 3:13.\x* when the command gave it an opportunity, and in doing so, it killed me.
\p
\v 12 Now the Law is indeed holy, and \add God’s\add* command is holy and righteous and good,
\v 13 so did what’s good turn into death? Not on your life! Rather, it was the good \add Law\add* that allowed me to see sin as sin and a producer of death, and the command showed me how completely sinful sin really is.
\s1 Doing what we know is wrong
\p
\v 14 We know that \add God’s\add* law is spiritual, but I am physical, having been \add born\add*\f + \fr 7:14 \ft Paul actually talks about ‘being sold into sin’ here which is slavery language.\f* into sin.
\v 15 I don’t know what’s going on, because I do what I don’t want to do, and I \add end up\add* doing what I hate.\x + \xo 7:15: \xt Gal 5:17.\x*
\v 16 \add So\add* if I do something that I didn’t want to do, then I’m agreeing with the Law that it’s good,
\v 17 and it’s no longer me that’s doing it, but the sin that’s in me.
\v 18 I’m aware that good isn’t in my physical body, because I want to do good but I can’t.
\v 19 I don’t do the good that I want to do, but rather I do the evil that I don’t want to do,
\v 20 so if I’m doing what I don’t want to do, then it’s not me doing it, but it’s the sin in me that does it.
\p
\v 21 As a result, I’ve discovered the principle that when I want to do good, \add actually\add* there’s evil in me,
\v 22 because I’m happily accepting \nd God’s\nd* Law on the inside,
\v 23 but I’m observing another law in my \add body parts\add*, fighting against my mind and keeping me imprisoned in sin’s law, which is in my \add body parts\add*.
\v 24 I’m a miserable person. Who’ll rescue me from this body that’s \add full of\add* death?
\v 25 Thankfully, it’s \nd God\nd*, through our \nd master Yeshua \+add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\+add* Messiah\nd*. So I end up with \add my\add* mind \add wanting to\add* serve \nd God’s\nd* Law, but on the other hand, \add my\add* body \add wants to serve\add* sin’s law.
\c 8
\s1 Being spiritually aware
\p
\v 1 So now those who are in \nd Messiah Yeshua\nd* \add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\add* \add cannot be condemned\add*,\f + \fr 8:1 \ft Note that many older translations included text here that came from manuscripts from later centuries and that isn’t there in the oldest manuscripts, i.e., the additional text appears to have been added by ‘helpful’ copyists but was not original to Shaul/Paul.\f*
\v 2 because the law of the \nd spirit\nd* of life in \add him\add* freed you from the law of sin and death.
\v 3 The law was powerless in that it was faltering through our bodies. \nd God\nd* sent his son in a body just like our sinful bodies, and concerning sin, he condemned sin \add itself\add* in the body,
\v 4 so that for those of us who are no longer governed by \add physical desires\add*, but by \nd the spirit\nd*, the law’s just action will be fulfilled in us.
\v 5 People who are governed by their physical desires think about physical things, but those who are governed by the \nd spirit\nd* \add think\add* about \nd spiritual\nd* things.
\v 6 The worldly mindset \add leads to\add* death, but the \nd spiritual\nd* mindset \add leads to\add* life and peace.
\v 7 The worldly mindset is making \nd God\nd* your enemy because it’s not being subjected to \nd God’s\nd* law, in fact it’s not even able to,
\v 8 and those who are worldly aren’t able \nd to\nd* \add do what pleases\add* \nd God\nd*.
\p
\v 9 And you, \add brothers and sisters,\add* aren’t worldly, but are in the \nd spirit\nd*, if indeed \nd God’s\nd* spirit is living in you, but anyone who doesn’t have \nd Messiah’s\nd* spirit doesn’t belong to him.
\v 10 But if \nd Messiah is\nd* in you, while \add your\add* body is dead because of sin, \add your\add* \nd spirit\nd* has life because it’s \add been declared\add* guiltless.
\v 11 \add Yes,\add* if the \nd spirit\nd* of the one who \add brought Yeshua\add* \add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\add* \add back to life\add* is living in you, then the \nd one\nd* who \add brought \+nd Messiah\+nd* Yeshua back to life\add* will also give life to your mortal bodies by means of his \nd spirit\nd* living inside you.\x + \xo 8:11: \xt 1Cor 3:16.\x*
\p
\v 12 So then, \add brothers and sisters\add*, we don’t owe anything to our sinful natures, nor do we need to live in worldly ways.
\v 13 If your lives, \add brothers and sisters\add*, \add are governed by\add* your sinful natures, you’ll die, but if you put bodily practices to death with \add the help of the\add* \nd spirit\nd*, you’ll live,
\v 14 because everyone who’s led \nd by God’s spirit\nd* \add becomes one of\add* \nd God’s\nd* children.
\v 15 You didn’t receive a spirit of slavery to fear again, but \add rather\add* it was \nd the spirit\nd* of adoption as children that you received, and \add with his help\add* we call out, ‘Dad, dad!’\x + \xo 8:15-17: \xt Gal 4:5-7.\x*
\v 16 \add God’s\add* \nd spirit\nd* himself agrees together with our \nd spirit\nd* that we are \nd God’s\nd* children,
\v 17 and if we’re \add his\add* children, then we’re also heirs, so we’ll receive an inheritance from \nd God\nd*. That makes us fellow-heirs along with \nd Messiah\nd*—if we’re suffering with him then we’ll also be honoured along with him.
\s1 Even creation itself is waiting
\p
\v 18 I’m sure that any sufferings \add we experience\add* in \add this age\add* are nothing in comparison with the coming splendour that’s going to be revealed to us,
\v 19 because \add all of\add* creation is eagerly expecting and waiting for \nd God’s\nd* children to \add be revealed\add*.
\v 20 \add Yes,\add* all of creation has been subjected to lack of purpose, not willingly, but because it was decided by the one who subjected it, but \add it remains with\add* the hope\x + \xo 8:20: \xt Gen 3:17-19.\x*
\v 21 that it too will be freed from the slavery of corruption, and then \add released\add* into the freedom of the splendour of \nd God’s\nd* children,
\v 22 because we’re aware that all of creation is groaning and suffering\f + \fr 8:22 \ft The word picture here is one of childbirth.\f* along with us right through to the present time.
\v 23 And not only that, but we ourselves who have the initial tastes of the \nd spirit\nd* \add life\add*, we’re also groaning and waiting like children for our \add bodies\add* to be \add set free from slavery\add*,\x + \xo 8:23: \xt 2Cor 5:2-4.\x*
\v 24 because we were saved, having hope, but if \add you\add* can see it then it’s not hope, because \add you\add* don’t need to hope for something \add you\add* can \add already\add* see.
\v 25 So we hope for what we can’t \add yet\add* see, and \add so\add* we have to patiently keep waiting.
\p
\v 26 Similarly, the \nd spirit\nd* also helps \add us\add* with our \add weaknesses\add*, because we don’t \add even\add* know what to pray for, but the \nd spirit\nd* intercedes with groanings that we can’t put into words.
\v 27 The one who searches our hearts knows the mindset of the \nd spirit\nd*, because the spirit intercedes for the believers as \nd God\nd* desires.
\p
\v 28 We know that \nd God\nd* makes all \add things\add* work together for good for everyone who loves \add him\add*—those who are being invited according to \add his\add* purpose.
\v 29 After all, those he knew in advance, he also determined in advance \add to be\add* similar to his son’s image, so that \add his son\add* would become the eldest of many siblings.
\v 30 The ones he’d determined in advance he also invited, and the ones he’d invited, he also declared guiltless, and the ones he declared guiltless, he also honoured \add in heaven\add*.
\s1 Nothing can separate us from God’s love
\p
\v 31 So then, what can we say about all this? If \nd God is\nd* for us, who’s against us?
\v 32 If he didn’t spare his own son, but gave him \add to be executed\add* for us, won’t they together also give us everything else?
\v 33 Who’ll bring charges against \add people\add* chosen by \nd God\nd*? \nd God is\nd* the one declaring \add us\add* guiltless.
\v 34 So who’s the one condemning \add us\add*? \nd Messiah Yeshua\nd* \add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\add* (the one who died but \add came back to life\add* and who’s \add now\add* \add sitting\add* beside \nd God\nd*) is also pleading \em for\em* us.\x + \xo 8:34: \xt Heb 7:25.\x*
\v 35 Who will separate us from \nd Messiah’s\nd* love? Troubles or distress or persecution or famine or poverty or danger or execution?
\v 36 As it’s been written:
\q1 ‘We are being put to death all day
\q2 on your account.
\q1 We were counted like sheep \add ready to be\add* slaughtered.’\x + \xo 8:36: \xt Psa 44:22.\x*
\m
\v 37 But in all that, we’re more than conquerors through the one who loved us.
\v 38 \add Yes,\add* I’ve been persuaded that neither death or life, angels or rulers, present or future events, powers,
\v 39 height or depth, or anything else in creation, will be able to separate us from \nd God’s\nd* love that’s \add revealed\add* in our \nd master Messiah Yeshua\nd*.
\c 9
\s1 God’s right to choose
\p
\v 1 I’m not lying—I’m speaking truthfully in \nd Messiah\nd*, and both my conscience and \nd the\nd* holy \nd spirit\nd* agree
\v 2 that my sorrow is extensive, and the sorrow in my heart just won’t stop,
\v 3 because I was hoping that I myself would be a sacrifice from the \nd messiah\nd* for my \add brothers and sisters\add*, my \add fellow-citizens\add* in this world.
\v 4 They’re the \add descendants\add* of Yisrael \add (Israel)\add* who’ve \add been adopted\add* as \add God’s\add* children,\x + \xo 9:4: \xt Exo 4:22.\x* and who \add God revealed\add* his power to, as well as the agreements, the Law, the \add temple\add* worship, and the promises.
\v 5 The patriarchs are \add Jewish\add*, and the \nd messiah\nd*, who \add has authority over\add* everything, \add physically\add* came from them. \add May \+nd God\+nd* be\add* blessed throughout the ages. \add May it be so\add*.
\p
\v 6 But it’s not that \nd God’s\nd* message has failed. Yisrael \add doesn’t include\add* all of Yisrael’s descendants,
\v 7 nor are all of Avraham’s descendants \add his\add* children, but, ‘It’s through Yitshak \add (Isaac)\add* that your descendants will be counted.’\x + \xo 9:7: \xt Gen 21:12.\x*
\v 8 That isn’t \add talking about\add* biological children—these are \nd God’s\nd* children, but the children of the promise are being counted as descendants.
\v 9 After all, this message is the promise: ‘I’ll come back, and Sarah will have a son.’\x + \xo 9:9: \xt Gen 18:10.\x*
\p
\v 10 And not only that, but also Rivkah \add (Rebecca)\add*, from one pregnancy to our ancestor Yitshak, delivered \add twins\add*.
\v 11 Before they were born, and \add so\add* before they could do anything good or bad, in order that \nd God’s\nd* deliberate choice would stand
\v 12 (not as a result of effort, but by \add God’s\add* announcement), she was told, ‘The older child will serve the younger one.’\x + \xo 9:12: \xt Gen 25:23.\x*
\v 13 As it was written: ‘I loved Ya’akov \add (Jacob)\add* but I hated Esav \add (Esau)\add*.’\x + \xo 9:13: \xt Mal 1:2-3.\x*
\p
\v 14 So what should we say then? \nd Is God\nd* unfair? Of course not,
\v 15 because he said to Mosheh \add (Moses)\add*: ‘I’ll be merciful to whoever I want to, and I’ll have pity on anyone I want to.’\x + \xo 9:15: \xt Exo 33:19.\x*
\v 16 In other words, it doesn’t depend on the willingness or activities of the person, but on \nd God’s\nd* mercy.
\v 17 After all, in the \add scriptures\add*, \add God\add* says to the king of Mitsrayim \add (Egypt)\add*: ‘I \add chose\add* you \add as king\add* so that I can demonstrate my power through you, and so that my name will be talked about all over the world.’\x + \xo 9:17: \xt Exo 9:16 (LXX).\x*
\v 18 In other words, \add God\add* will be merciful to whoever he wants to, but he hardens whoever he wants to.
\s1 God’s anger and his mercy
\p
\v 19 So now you’ll ask me: Then how can \add God\add* blame us, because how could anyone resist whatever he wants to do?
\v 20 Oh human, are you talking back \nd to God\nd*? Can the creature that was made talk back to the maker and ask: ‘Why did you make me like this?’\x + \xo 9:20: \xt Isa 29:16; 45:9.\x*
\v 21 Doesn’t the potter have the right to split the lump of clay and make a beautiful bowl out of half and a \add chamber pot\add* out of the other half?
\p
\v 22 \add What\add* if \nd God\nd* wanted to display his severe anger and to demonstrate his power, but then showed great patience to the objects of \add his\add* severe anger that he’d prepared for destruction?
\v 23 \add What if he did that\add* in order to make the riches of his greatness known to the objects of \add his\add* mercy that he’d prepared for honour?
\v 24 \add Yes,\add* he even called us, not only from \add among\add* the Yehudim \add (Jews)\add* but also from non-Jews.
\v 25 The \add prophet\add* Hoshea \add (Hosea)\add* even wrote:
\q1 “Those who aren’t my people
\q2 I will call ‘my people’,
\q1 and the \add woman\add* who was never loved
\q2 is the one I’ll call ‘my love’.\x + \xo 9:25: \xt Hos 2:23.\x*
\q1
\v 26 In the place where they said,
\q2 ‘You’re not my people’
\q1 that’s where they’ll be called ‘children \nd of\nd* the living \nd God\nd*’.”\x + \xo 9:26: \xt Hos 1:10.\x*
\m
\v 27 \add The prophet\add* Yeshayah \add (Isaiah)\add* announced about Yisrael \add (Israel)\add*: ‘Even though the Israelis are as numerous as grains of sand, \add only\add* a minority \add of them\add* will be saved,
\v 28 because \nd Yahweh\nd* will be abbreviating and terminating his message on the earth.’\x + \xo 9:27-28: \xt Isa 10:22-23 (LXX).\x*
\v 29 As Yeshayah had previously written: ‘If Commander-in-chief \nd Yahweh\nd* hadn’t left us \add some\add* descendants, we would have become like Sedom and Amorah’s \add (Gomorrah)\add* \add people groups\add*.’\x + \xo 9:29: \xt Isa 1:9 (LXX).\x*
\s1 The Yehudim’ failure to believe
\p
\v 30 So then, what should we say? That non-Jews who weren’t godly reached out by faith to achieve righteousness,
\v 31 but Yehudim \add (Jews)\add* who chased after righteousness \add by obeying\add* The Law didn’t achieve it?
\v 32 How come? Because \add they tried to reach it\add* by actions and not by faith. They tripped over the stumbling stone,
\v 33 as it’s written:
\q1 ‘Look, I’m laying down a stumbling stone in Tsiyon \add (Zion)\add*,
\q2 a rock \add that causes\add* offence.
\q1 Anyone who believes in him
\q2 won’t be disgraced.’\x + \xo 9:33: \xt Isa 28:16 (LXX).\x*
\c 10
\p
\v 1 \add Brothers and sisters\add*, my heart would be very pleased indeed if \add Israelis\add* would get \add rescued\add*, and that’s what I request from \nd God\nd*.
\v 2 I keep telling them that they’re \add very\add* zealous \nd for God\nd*, yet they lack know-how.
\v 3 Because they didn’t know what \nd God’s\nd* righteousness is, and they tried to establish their own \add system\add*, they weren’t covered by \add his\add* righteousness.
\v 4 The fulfilment of The Law is \nd Messiah\nd*, \add leading to\add* righteousness for everyone who believes.
\s1 Salvation is for all peoples
\p
\v 5 Mosheh \add (Moses)\add* wrote this about the Law’s righteousness: ‘The person who practices it will live because of it.’\x + \xo 10:5: \xt Lev 18:5.\x*
\v 6 But the righteousness \add based on\add* faith says: ‘Don’t \add speculate\add* in your \add mind\add* about who’ll go up to heaven in order to bring \nd Messiah\nd* down,
\v 7 and don’t ask about who’ll go down to the pit in order to bring \nd Messiah\nd* up.’
\v 8 So what does the righteousness \add based on\add* faith say? \add It says:\add* ‘The message is close to you—it’s in your mouth and heart.’\x + \xo 10:6-8: \xt Deu 30:12-14.\x* That’s the message of faith that we’ve been preaching:
\v 9 That if you speak out and acknowledge that \nd Yeshua\nd* \add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\add* is \nd Yahweh\nd*, and you believe in your heart that \nd God\nd* \add brought him back to life\add* from the dead, you will be rescued,
\v 10 because it’s the belief in \add your\add* \add mind\add* that leads to righteousness, and it’s the \add acknowledgement\add* from \add your\add* mouth that leads to rescue.
\v 11 As the \add scriptures\add* say: ‘Anyone who believes in him won’t \add end up\add* disgraced.’\x + \xo 10:11: \xt Isa 28:16 (LXX).\x*
\v 12 There’s no distinction between Yehudi \add (Jew)\add* and non-Jew, because \add we\add* all have the same \nd master\nd* who gives generously to everyone who makes an appeal to him,
\v 13 because everyone who appeals to \nd Yahweh’s\nd* \add authority\add* will be rescued.\x + \xo 10:13: \xt Yoel 2:32.\x*
\p
\v 14 But how could they appeal to a god that they don’t \add even\add* believe in? And how could they believe if they haven’t \add even\add* heard \add about him\add*? And how would they hear if no one told \add them\add*?
\v 15 And who would be \add there\add* telling \add them\add* if no one was sent out? \add Compare\add* what was written: ‘It’s so wonderful that these people came and announced the good message.’\x + \xo 10:15: \xt Isa 52:7.\x*
\v 16 But not everyone accepted the good message, just like \add the prophet\add* Yeshayah \add (Isaiah)\add* wrote: ‘\nd Yahweh\nd*, who believed our report?’\x + \xo 10:16: \xt Isa 53:1 (LXX).\x*
\v 17 And so faith comes by hearing—\add listening to\add* \nd Messiah’s\nd* message.
\p
\v 18 But then I’d ask: Haven’t they heard \add it\add*? Indeed they have. \add It’s written:\add*
\q1 ‘Their voice went out into all the earth,
\q1 and their messages went across all the world.’\x + \xo 10:18: \xt Psa 19:4 (LXX).\x*
\m
\v 19 But \add again\add* I’d ask: Didn’t the Israelis know? \add Well,\add* firstly Mosheh \add (Moses)\add* wrote:
\q1 ‘I’ll be making you all jealous
\q2 using \add people\add* who are not even a nation.
\q1 Then using a foolish nation,
\q2 I’ll be making you all angry.’\x + \xo 10:19: \xt Deu 32:21.\x*
\m
\v 20 And Yeshayah \add (Isaiah)\add* dared to write:
\q1 ‘The ones who weren’t \add looking for\add* me found me.
\q1 I showed myself to the ones not asking \add about me\add*.’\x + \xo 10:20: \xt Isa 65:1 (LXX).\x*
\m
\v 21 But to Yisrael \add (Israel)\add* he wrote: ‘All day I stretched out my hands to a people who won’t believe and who speak against \add me\add*.’\x + \xo 10:21: \xt Isa 65:2 (LXX).\x*
\c 11
\s1 God’s mercy on the Yehudim
\p
\v 1 Therefore I’m asking: Did \nd God\nd* push his \add chosen\add* people away? \add Not at all\add*, because I’m an Israeli, too, a descendant of Avraham in the tribe of Benyamin.\x + \xo 11:1: \xt Php 3:5.\x*
\v 2 \add No,\add* \nd God\nd* didn’t push away his people, who he knew before \add he chose them\add*. Or don’t you know what scripture says about Eliyah \add (Elijah)\add* pleading \nd with God\nd* to deal with Yisrael \add (Israel)\add*? \add He said:\add*
\v 3 ‘\nd Yahweh\nd*, they killed your prophets and tore down your altars, and I was left alone, and \add now\add* they want to kill me!’\x + \xo 11:3: \xt 1Ki 19:10,14.\x*
\v 4 But what \add was revealed\add* to him? \add God said:\add* ‘I’ve kept aside seven thousand others who haven’t worshipped Ba’al.’\x + \xo 11:4: \xt 1Ki 19:18.\x*
\v 5 So too, at the present time, there’s a small proportion who’ve been chosen by \add God’s\add* generosity.
\v 6 So if it’s by generosity, then it’s no longer by \add their\add* actions, otherwise the generosity wouldn’t be generosity \add at all\add*.
\p
\v 7 What’s \add the conclusion\add* then? The Israelis didn’t find what they were \add looking for\add*. Only the chosen ones reached it, and the rest were hardened,
\v 8 just like it was written:
\q1 ‘\nd God\nd* gave them a lethargic spirit:
\q1 Eyes which can’t see and ears which can’t hear,
\q2 \add even\add* to this very day.’\x + \xo 11:8: \xt Deu 29:4; Isa 29:10.\x*
\p
\v 9 And as David wrote:
\q1 ‘Let their table become a trap for them—
\q2 a temptation that will pay them back.
\q1
\v 10 Let their eyes be darkened so they can’t see,
\q1 and their \add backs\add* be bent through all time.’\x + \xo 11:9-10: \xt Psa 69:22-23 (LXX).\x*
\p
\v 11 So now I ask: didn’t they stumble so that they would fall? Not at all. But because of their disobedience, salvation came to non-Jews in order to make \add Yehudim\add* \add (Jews)\add* envious.
\v 12 But if their disobedience \add led to\add* gain for the \add rest of the\add* world, and if their loss \add led to\add* gain for the non-Jews, how much more their fullness?
\s1 The grafting in of non-Jews
\p
\v 13 But \add now\add* I’m speaking to you non-Jews. Since I am indeed an ambassador to the non-Jews, I \add take pride in\add* my service,
\v 14 hoping that somehow I might make my fellow Yehudim \add (Jews)\add* envious and might save some of them.
\v 15 After all, if their rejection \add of the messiah\add* meant that \add the rest of\add* the world would be reconciled, what else could their acceptance bring other than \add coming back to life\add* from the dead?
\p
\v 16 If the first portion \add of dough\add* is holy, so is the lump, and if the root is holy, so are the branches.
\v 17 But if some of the branches were broken off, and you \add non-Jews\add*, who are like a wild olive tree, were grafted into them, and so you too were able to share in the olive tree’s growth, which \add came from\add* the root,
\v 18 then don’t brag that you’re better than the branches. If you must boast, remember that you’re not supporting the root, but the root is supporting you.
\p
\v 19 \add Ah,\add* but then you’ll say, ‘Branches were broken off so that we could be grafted in.’
\v 20 \add Well\add* you’re right. They were broken off because of their disbelief, and \add it’s true\add* that you’re there because of \add your\add* faith. But don’t get a big head—maintain your reverence \add for God\add*,
\v 21 because if \add he\add* didn’t spare the natural branches \add when they showed disbelief\add*, then he won’t spare you either.
\v 22 So you can see both \nd God’s\nd* kindness and his severity—those who disobeyed \add experienced\add* his severity, but you \add experienced\add* \add his\add* kindness. Otherwise you’ll be pruned off, too,
\v 23 and also, if they turn away from their disbelief, they’ll be grafted \add back\add* in, because \nd God\nd* has the power to do that.
\v 24 If you were cut off an olive tree that’s \em wild\em* by nature, and contrary to normal practice were grafted into a \em cultivated\em* olive tree, how much more \add easily\add* would those who naturally belong be grafted into their own olive tree!
\s1 God’s mercy is for everyone
\p
\v 25 \add Brothers and sisters\add*, I don’t want you to be \add ignorant\add* of this mystery, \add otherwise you might\add* end up becoming proud: There’s a partial hardening coming to the Yehudim \add (Jews)\add* until the fullness of the non-Jews comes,
\v 26 and then all the Yehudim will be saved, as it’s been written:
\q1 ‘The rescuer will be coming out of Tsiyon \add (Zion)\add*.
\q1 He will drive ungodliness away from Ya’akov \add (Jacob)\add*.
\q1
\v 27 And this is my agreement with them,\x + \xo 11:26-27: \xt Isa 59:20-21 (LXX).\x*
\q1 whenever I might take away their sins.’\x + \xo 11:27: \xt Isa 27:9 (LXX).\x*
\m
\v 28 Concerning the good message, \add the Yehudim are\add* enemies for your sake, but concerning being chosen, \add they’re\add* loved because of \add their\add* ancestors,
\v 29 because \nd God’s\nd* gifts and his invitation are irrevocable.
\v 30 You \add non-Jews\add* didn’t originally believe \nd in God\nd*, but now you’ve been shown mercy because of the disbelief of these \add Yehudim\add*.
\v 31 Those \add Yehudim\add* disbelieved so that as you \add receive\add* mercy, they can also now be shown mercy,
\v 32 because \nd God\nd* imprisoned them all in disbelief so that he could show mercy to everyone.
\s1 Everything comes from God
\p
\v 33 \add Yes,\add* \nd God’s\nd* wisdom and knowledge are both so richly deep. His judgements can’t be understood and his paths can’t be mapped,\x + \xo 11:33: \xt Isa 55:8.\x*
\v 34 because:
\q1 ‘Who can know \nd Yahweh’s\nd* mind
\q1 or become his counsellor?’\x + \xo 11:34: \xt Isa 40:13 (LXX).\x*
\q1
\v 35 ‘Who has been able to give him something,
\q2 so that \add God\add* \add now\add* needs to repay them?’\x + \xo 11:35: \xt Yob 41:11.\x*
\m
\v 36 \add Yes,\add* because everything comes from \add God\add*, and everything comes through him and to him.\x + \xo 11:36: \xt 1Cor 8:6.\x* May he be honoured throughout the ages. \add May it be so\add*.
\c 12
\s1 Different ways to serve ‘the body’
\p
\v 1 So then, \add brothers and sisters\add*, with \add compassion\add* from \nd God\nd* I urge you to present your bodies as a living sacrifice—holy \add and\add* pleasing \nd to God\nd*—your logical \add and\add* sacred service.
\v 2 Don’t conform to \add the norms of\add* this age, but be transformed by the renewing of \add your\add* \add minds\add*, then you’ll be able to \add accept and\add* approve \nd God’s\nd* good, pleasing, and perfect intentions.
\p
\v 3 Because of the generosity which has been shown to me, I’m saying that \add none of you should think of yourself more highly\add* than what’s appropriate, but rather \add you should\add* be sensible about what you think of yourselves according to how much faith \nd God’s\nd* allotted to each of you.
\v 4 Just like one body has many components, and each individual component works differently,\x + \xo 12:4-5: \xt 1Cor 12:12.\x*
\v 5 so too we’re all part of one body in \nd Messiah\nd* and we’re all parts of each other.
\v 6 \add We\add* have gifts according to the special generosity that was shown to us:\x + \xo 12:6-8: \xt 1Cor 12:4-11.\x* if it’s prophecy, \add then do it\add* in proportion to \add your\add* faith,
\v 7 or if it’s service, \add then\add* serve, or teaching, \add then\add* teach,
\v 8 or encouraging, \add then\add* encourage, or sharing, \add then\add* \add be generous\add*, or leading, \add then do it\add* \add earnestly\add*, or showing mercy, \add then do it\add* \add cheerfully\add*.
\p
\v 9 Your love is to be free from hypocrisy. Deplore evil, and be united in doing good.
\v 10 Be devoted to one another in brotherly love, preferring to honour one another \add above yourselves\add*.
\v 11 Be diligent, rather than reluctant, with a spirit that’s zealous to serve the \nd master\nd*.
\v 12 Be cheerful about \add your\add* hope, enduring persecution and consistently praying.
\v 13 Contribute to the needs of the believers and practice hospitality.
\p
\v 14 Bless anyone who persecutes you—be a blesser and not a curser.\x + \xo 12:14: \xt Mat 5:44; Luk 6:28.\x*
\v 15 Be cheerful along with \add others who are\add* happy, but weep along with \add those who are\add* weeping.
\v 16 Treat others equally—not treating some as higher, and being condescending to those who are poorer. Don’t \add act in a calculating way\add*.\x + \xo 12:16: \xt Prv 3:7.\x*
\p
\v 17 Don’t \add pay back anyone’s evil with evil\add*, but do good to everyone.
\v 18 If it’s possible for you, \add brothers and sisters\add*, be at peace with everyone.
\v 19 Dear friends, don’t \add take revenge\add* on others, but leave it to \add God’s\add* severe anger, because it’s written: ‘\nd Yahweh\nd* says, Vengeance belongs to me—I’ll be \nd the\nd* one to repay.’\x + \xo 12:19: \xt Deu 32:35.\x*
\v 20 But if your \add enemies\add* are hungry, then feed them, and if they’re thirsty, then give them a drink, because you’ll be leaving them to their own shame and judgement.\x + \xo 12:20: \xt Prv 25:21-22 (LXX).\x*
\v 21 Don’t be overcome by evil, but overcome evil with good.
\c 13
\s1 Respect authorities and pay tax
\p
\v 1 Everyone should be subject to the authorities who are over them, because there’s no authority except through \nd God\nd*, and they’re appointed by \nd God\nd*.
\v 2 So anyone opposing authority has rebelled against what \nd God\nd* has instituted, and everyone who rebels is bringing judgement on themselves,
\v 3 because rulers don’t evoke fear in \add people\add* doing good, just those doing evil. Oh, so you don’t want to be afraid of the \add authorities\add*? Then do good, and you’ll receive praise from them \add instead\add*,
\v 4 because they’re \nd God’s\nd* servants for your good. But if you’re doing evil, then you should \add rightly\add* be afraid, because the authorities aren’t armed without reason—they’re \nd God’s\nd* servants to dispense severe anger to \add people\add* doing evil.
\v 5 Hence it’s \add necessary\add* to be subject to them, not only \add to avoid\add* \add their\add* severe anger, but also because of \add your\add* \add consciences\add*.
\p
\v 6 That’s also why you \add believers\add* should pay the taxes that you owe,\x + \xo 13:6-7: \xt Mat 22:21; Mrk 12:17; Luk 20:25.\x* because \add the authorities\add* are \nd God’s\nd* servants, and they work hard at it.
\v 7 So fulfil all of \add your\add* obligations: if it’s tax then pay it, or revenue then give it, or if it’s respect then show it, or giving honour then do it.
\s1 Love and obedience
\p
\v 8 Don’t be in debt to anyone, other than the debt of love to each other, because \add people\add* who love each other have fulfilled \add God’s\add* law,
\v 9 which is: Don’t commit adultery.\x + \xo 13:9: \xt Exo 20:14; Deu 5:18.\x* Don’t murder.\x + \xo 13:9: \xt Exo 20:13; Deu 5:17.\x* Don’t steal.\x + \xo 13:9: \xt Exo 20:15; Deu 5:19.\x* Don’t covet \add what others have\add*.\x + \xo 13:9: \xt Exo 20:17; Deu 5:21.\x* All of those, plus any other \add commands\add*, can be summarised as: Love your neighbour like \add you love\add* yourself.\x + \xo 13:9: \xt Lev 19:18.\x*
\v 10 If you love \add people\add* near to you, you won’t do evil to them, thus love fulfills \add God’s\add* law.
\p
\v 11 And since you \add believers\add* understand \add current events\add*, it’s now time to get up from your sleep, because the time for our rescue is nearer now than it was when we first believed.
\v 12 The night has progressed and the day is getting closer, so turn away from actions done in darkness and \add put on the armour\add* of the light.
\v 13 In the daytime we have to walk decently—not carousing \add on the street\add* and \add being drunk\add*, not having sex and acting lustfully, and not causing strife and acting jealously.
\v 14 Instead, dress yourselves in the \nd master Yeshua \+add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\+add* Messiah\nd*, and don’t give in to physical lusts and desires.
\c 14
\s1 Don’t condemn each other
\p
\v 1 Accept others who are faltering in \add their\add* faith, without highlighting their wrong ideas.\x + \xo 14:1-6: \xt Col 2:16.\x*
\v 2 Some people think it’s ok to eat anything, while others who are faltering will only eat vegetables.
\v 3 The one who eats anything shouldn’t despise the other, and the one who doesn’t eat \add meat\add* shouldn’t condemn the other, because \add both\add* are accepted by \nd God\nd*.
\v 4 Who are you to judge someone else’s house \add servants\add*? Their own master will judge if they stand or fall, and they’ll stand because the master is able to \add help\add* them.
\p
\v 5 \add Similarly,\add* some people consider one day to be more important than some other day, yet others consider every day to be alike. Each \add person\add* should be fully convinced in their own mind.
\v 6 Anyone who considers a \add certain\add* day to be special is thinking about \nd Yahweh\nd*. \add Likewise,\add* anyone who’s eating \add meat\add* is eating to \add serve\add* Yahweh, and gives thanks to \nd God\nd*. The person who doesn’t eat \add meat\add* is declining \add it in order to serve\add* Yahweh, and gives thanks \nd to God\nd*.
\v 7 \add After all,\add* we don’t live for ourselves, and we don’t die for ourselves.
\v 8 If we keep living, then we’re living for the \nd master\nd*, and if we die, then we’re dying for the \nd master\nd*, so whether we live or die, we belong to the \nd master\nd*,
\v 9 because \nd Messiah\nd* died and \add came back to life\add* in order to be master of both the dead and the living.
\v 10 So why are you judging fellow believers? And why are you also scorning them? \add After all,\add* we’ll all \add end up\add* standing in \nd God’s\nd* court \nd of\nd* judgement.\x + \xo 14:10: \xt 2Cor 5:10.\x*
\v 11 As it’s been written:
\q1 “\nd Yahweh\nd* is telling me, ‘I am alive
\q2 and every person will bow to me,
\q1 and each one will confess \nd to God\nd*.’ ”\x + \xo 14:11: \xt Isa 45:23 (LXX).\x*
\m
\v 12 So consequently, each of us will \add need\add* to give an account of ourselves \nd to God\nd*.
\s1 Taking care not to make others fall
\p
\v 13 So we shouldn’t be judging each other, but instead we should be judging \add ourselves\add*—that we’re not causing another believer to stumble or \add fall into\add* temptation.
\v 14 I know (and I’m \add totally\add* persuaded) that in \nd master Yeshua\nd* \add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\add* there’s no \add object\add* that’s ‘immoral’\f + \fr 14:14 \ft Or (traditionally) ‘unclean’.\f* in itself, but it only becomes ‘immoral’ to the person who evaluates it as such.
\v 15 If a fellow believer is upset because of the food \add you eat\add*, then you’re no longer acting in love. Don’t destroy \add the faith of\add* \nd someone\nd* that \nd Messiah\nd* died for, \add just\add* for the sake of food.
\v 16 Don’t let anyone slander the good that you do.
\v 17 \nd God’s\nd* kingdom doesn’t \add consist of\add* food and drink, but \add of\add* righteousness, peace, and happiness in the holy \nd spirit\nd*,
\v 18 so \nd anyone\nd* serving the \nd messiah\nd* in those ways pleases \nd God\nd* and \nd is\nd* approved by the people.
\p
\v 19 So then, we should be pursuing actions that lead to peace and things that build each other up—
\v 20 don’t tear down \nd God’s\nd* work \add just\add* on account \nd of\nd* food. Indeed, all foods are ‘clean,’\f + \fr 14:20 \ft In Hebrew, ‘kosher’.\f* but it’s wrong for a person to eat anything that \add causes someone else\add* to stumble.
\v 21 It’s good to avoid eating \add meat\add* or drinking wine or \add doing\add* anything else that \add might\add* cause your fellow believer to stumble or be offended or fall.
\v 22 \add That way,\add* you keep your faith \add privately, between yourself and God\add*. \add God\add* will bless anyone who doesn’t bring guilt on themselves by doing something that they’ve determined to be right.
\v 23 But for anyone who doubts that it’s ok, if they eat it, then they’ve condemned themselves, because they’re not acting in faith, and everything that’s not done in faith is sin.
\c 15
\s1 Living to please others
\p
\v 1 We who are powerful should be carrying the weaknesses of the powerless, rather than \add just\add* trying to please ourselves.
\v 2 Each one of us should be pleasing \add those\add* near \add us\add*—displaying goodness and building \add them\add* up,
\v 3 because even the \nd messiah\nd* didn’t please himself, but, as it was written: ‘Those who were insulting you, their insults landed on me.’\x + \xo 15:3: \xt Psa 69:9.\x*
\v 4 Everything that was written in the past was written for teaching us, so that through endurance and through encouragement from the scriptures, we would have hope.
\v 5 May the \nd God\nd* of endurance and encouragement help you all to think likewise, just like \nd Yeshua \+add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\+add* Messiah\nd* did,
\v 6 so that you’ll be united in your purpose and your teaching, thus honouring the \nd God\nd* and father of our \nd master\nd*, \nd Yeshua Messiah\nd*.
\s1 The good message for non-Jews
\p
\v 7 So then, accept each other just as the \nd messiah\nd* accepted you, so that \nd God\nd* will be honoured.
\v 8 I can tell you that \nd Messiah\nd* became a servant of the \add circumcised\add* \add Yehudim\add* \add (Jews)\add* so that \nd God’s\nd* truth \add will be seen by the\add* \add confirmation\add* \nd of\nd* the promises that were made to \add our\add* ancestors,
\v 9 and \add also a servant\add* for the non-Jews so that they’ll honour \nd God\nd* for \add his\add* mercy, as it was written:
\q1 ‘Because of this I’ll be telling the pagans about you,
\q1 and I’ll be singing praises to your name.’\x + \xo 15:9: \xt 2Sam 22:50; Psa 18:49.\x*
\m
\v 10 \add In a different place\add* it’s written:
\q1 ‘You non-Jews, be happy along with his people.’\x + \xo 15:10: \xt Deu 32:43.\x*
\m
\v 11 And again:
\q1 ‘Praise \nd Yahweh\nd* you non-Jews,
\q1 and let all the peoples \add of the world\add* praise him.’\x + \xo 15:11: \xt Psa 117:1.\x*
\m
\v 12 Also Yeshayah \add (Isaiah)\add* wrote:
\q1 ‘There will be a descendant of Yishay \add (Jesse)\add*,
\q1 and the one coming to rule over non-Jews—
\q2 the non-Jews will \add put their hope in\add* him.’\x + \xo 15:12: \xt Isa 11:10 (LXX).\x*
\p
\v 13 May the \nd God\nd* of hope that you believe in fill you \add believers\add* with happiness and peace, so that your hope in the power \nd of the\nd* holy \nd spirit\nd* will \add grow to be\add* plentiful.
\s1 Paulos as missionary to non-Jews
\p
\v 14 My \add brothers and sisters\add*, I’ve also been persuaded about you myself, that having been filled with knowledge, you’re also full of goodness and able to rebuke each other.
\v 15 But I wrote to you more boldly, partly to remind you, \add brothers and sisters,\add* because of the generosity \nd God\nd* \add showed\add* me
\v 16 so that I’d \add serve\add* the non-Jews for \nd Messiah Yeshua\nd* \add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\add*, sharing \nd God’s\nd* good message so that the offering of the non-Jews would be acceptable \add to him\add*, having been purified by \nd the\nd* holy \nd spirit\nd*. WHAT DOES THIS MEAN?
\v 17 Therefore I’m boasting in \nd Messiah Yeshua\nd* about things \add to do with\add* \nd God\nd*.
\v 18 I wouldn’t dare to speak about anything \add except\add* what \nd Messiah\nd* did through me, which led to the obedience of non-Jews through \add my\add* message and \add through my\add* doing
\v 19 powerful miracles and amazing things in the power \nd of God’s spirit\nd*, so that from Yerushalem \add (Jerusalem)\add* all the way around to Illurikon, I’ve obeyed the \nd Messiah’s\nd* good message.
\v 20 I was eager to be preaching the good message wherever \nd Messiah\nd* wasn’t known about, so that I wouldn’t be building on \nd someone\nd* else’s \add foundations\add*,
\v 21 but as it was written:
\q1 ‘Anyone who hasn’t been told about him,
\q2 they will be seeing,
\q1 and anyone who hasn’t heard about him,
\q2 they will be understanding.’\x + \xo 15:21: \xt Isa 52:15 (LXX).\x*
\s1 Paulos’s desire to visit Romai
\p
\v 22 That’s also why I \add wasn’t able to come\add* \add to see\add* you \add Romaios,\add*\x + \xo 15:22: \xt Rom 1:13.\x*
\v 23 but now I no longer have a role in those regions. I’ve been wanting to come there for many years now—
\v 24 whenever I’m able to go to Spania next, I’m hoping to see you on my way, and then after \add spending time\add* with you \add Romaios\add*, you’ll be able to send me on to my destination.
\v 25 But for now, I’m going to Yerushalem \add (Jerusalem)\add* to serve the believers there.\x + \xo 15:25-26: \xt 1Cor 16:1-4.\x*
\v 26 \add The believers in\add* Makedonia and Akaia took pleasure in making a contribution for the poor \add people\add* among the believers \add there\add* in Yerushalem.
\v 27 They took pleasure, \add but\add* they’re also in debt to them, because if the non-Jews have shared in \add Jewish\add* spiritual \add things\add*, then they should also \add support\add* them with material \add things\add*.\x + \xo 15:27: \xt 1Cor 9:11.\x*
\v 28 So then, having done that, and having guaranteed \add heavenly rewards\add* for them, I’ll go to Spania by way of your \add place\add*,
\v 29 and I know that when I come, I’ll come \add bearing\add* \nd Messiah’s\nd* full blessing.
\p
\v 30 \add So\add* \add brothers and sisters\add*, I’m urging you by our \nd master Yeshua \+add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\+add* Messiah\nd* and by the \nd spirit’s\nd* love, to strive along with me in praying to \nd God\nd* for me—
\v 31 that I’ll escape from \add the schemes of\add* those in Yehudah \add (Judea)\add* who refuse to believe. Also pray that my service in Yerushalem would be acceptable to the believers there
\v 32 so that by \nd God’s\nd* will, I can happily come to your place and be refreshed \add there\add* with you, \add brothers and sisters\add*.
\v 33 \add May\add* the \nd God\nd* of peace be with all of you. \add May it be so\add*.
\c 16
\s1 Various personal greetings
\p
\v 1 I’m commending our sister Foibe \add (Phoebe)\add* to you, \add brothers and sisters\add*—she’s a servant in the assembly at Kegkreai.
\v 2 You can accept her in \nd the master\nd* as worthy among godly people, and help her with anything she needs, because she’s been a supporter of many of us, including myself.
\p
\v 3 Greet Priskilla and Akulas \add (Aquila)\add*,\x + \xo 16:3: \xt Acts 18:2.\x* my workmates in \nd Messiah Yeshua\nd* \add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\add*,
\v 4 who risked their lives to save mine. It’s not just me who’s thankful for them, but also all the assemblies of the non-Jews
\v 5 and the assembly \add that meets\add* in their house.
\p Greet my dear \add friend\add* Epainetos, who was \nd one\nd* of the first in \add the province of\add* Asia \add Minor\add* to \add follow\add* \nd Messiah\nd*.
\v 6 Greet Miryam \add (Grk. Maria/Mariam)\add*, who worked hard for you, \add brothers and sisters\add*.
\v 7 Greet Andronikos and \add our sister\add* Yunia, my fellow citizens and fellow prisoners with me, who are notable among the ambassadors in \nd Messiah\nd* who \add started\add* before me.
\p
\v 8 Greet Ampliatos, my dear \add friend\add* in \nd the master\nd*.
\v 9 Greet Urbanos, our fellow worker in \nd Messiah\nd*, and my dear \add friend\add* Stakus.
\v 10 Greet Apelles, who’s a proven follower of \nd Messiah\nd*. Greet those from Aristobulos’s \add household\add*.
\v 11 Greet Herodion, my fellow citizen. Greet those in Narkissos’s \add household\add* who are in \nd the master\nd*.
\p
\v 12 Greet Trufaina and Trufosa, those \add women\add* who are working for \nd the master\nd*. Greet my dear \add friend\add* Persis, another woman who’s worked for \nd the master\nd* a lot.
\v 13 Greet Rufos,\x + \xo 16:13: \xt Mrk 15:21.\x* who’s chosen by \nd the master\nd*, and his mother, who also mothered me.
\v 14 Greet Asunkritos, Flegon, Hermes, Patrobas, Hermas, and the other \add brothers and sisters\add* with them.
\v 15 Greet Filologos, Yulia, Nereus and his sister, and Olumpas, and all the godly people \add there\add* with them.
\p
\v 16 Greet each other with a traditional kiss. All the \nd messiah’s\nd* assemblies \add here\add* send their greetings to you, \add brothers and sisters\add*.
\s1 The ending warnings
\p
\v 17 \add Brothers and sisters\add*, I urge you to beware of those who are \add always\add* talking about faults and \add tempting\add* you with things that are against what you’ve been taught. Turn away from them,
\v 18 because such people are serving their own \add bellies\add* instead of our \nd master Messiah\nd*, and they’re deceiving innocent \add people\add* with \add their\add* smooth speech and ‘\add blessings\add*’.
\v 19 I’m excited because the \add story\add* of your obedience has travelled far, but I want you \add believers\add* to \add remain\add* wise about what’s good, and innocent about evil,
\v 20 then the \nd God\nd* of peace will \add quickly\add* crush Satan under your feet.\x + \xo 16:20: \xt Gen 3:15.\x*
\s1 Final greetings
\p \add May\add* our \nd master Yeshua’s\nd* \add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\add* generosity be with you \add believers\add*.
\p
\v 21 My co-worker Timotheos\x + \xo 16:21: \xt Acts 16:1.\x* sends his greetings, as do my fellow citizens Lukas \add (Luke)\add*, Yason, and Sosipatros.
\p
\v 22 I, Tertios, the one writing down this letter, greet you \add believers\add* in \nd the master\nd*.
\p
\v 23 My host Gaios\x + \xo 16:23: \xt Acts 19:29; 1Cor 1:14.\x* and the whole assembly \add here\add* greet you \add believers\add*.\x + \xo 16:23: \xt 2Tim 4:20.\x*
\s1 Closing
\p
\v 24 ◘
\v 25 \add There’s\add* \nd someone\nd* who, in keeping with my preaching of the good message and the declaration \nd about Yeshua \+add (Jesus; Grk. Yesus)\+add* Messiah\nd*, is able to strengthen you \add believers\add* in keeping with the revelation of the mystery that had been kept secret through the ages.
\v 26 But he’s been \add there\add* in the prophetic scriptures, and now he’s been revealed in keeping with the eternal \nd God’s\nd* command, and been made known to all the nations for faithful obedience.
\v 27 \add Now\add* may the only wise \nd God\nd* be honoured through \nd Yeshua Messiah\nd* throughout the \add remaining\add* ages. \add May it be so\add*.
